          "contents": [
            {
              "name": "§7.1: sample-size goals depend on the system, target effect or interval width, error variance, and desired power; sample-size selection searches for the smallest allocation meeting the goal.",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "def-power-and-sample-size-planning-specification"
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.1's smallest-size criterion supports the local well-ordering discharge and the distinction between an extensional minimum and a finite search procedure.",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "lem-least-feasible-sample-size-exists"
            },
            {
              "name": "§§7.3–7.4: power is rejection probability under a stated alternative; exact power depends on the test's alternative distribution, and a minimum sample size is selected by testing candidate sizes.",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "alg-finite-candidate-power-sample-size-search"
            },
            {
              "name": "§§7.3–7.4 use model-specific power calculations and a smallest-sample-size search; their ANOVA/contrast laws are not imported into the local one-sample z-test proof.",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "thm-finite-candidate-power-sample-size-search-correctness"
            },
            {
              "name": "§§7.3–7.4: model-specific power and sample-size calculations for ANOVA and contrasts; supports keeping test law and alternative explicit.",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "thm-known-variance-normal-z-test-power-and-minimum-sample-size"
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.1–§7.4 use normal critical values and distinguish power calculations by sidedness and test law; the CDF regularity and quantile existence claims are proved locally.",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "lem-standard-normal-cdf-quantiles-and-tail-symmetry-for-power"
            },
            {
              "name": "§§7.3–7.4 show why a two-sided power calculation must be tied to its stated test and alternative distribution; the two normal tails are proved locally.",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "prop-known-variance-normal-two-sided-z-test-power"
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.2 concerns interval-width planning; §7.6 warns allocations depend on the contrast and cost structure. These passages support the planning inputs, not a universal width or allocation formula.",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "def-power-and-sample-size-planning-specification"
